        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <p>OBJECTIVES:<p>
Metamorphosis in biological species provides a unique condition for studying the role of
gravity in development. Formation of new organs in a previously existing organism requires
a highly active period of turnover of amino acids and proteins, and of changes in the
endocrine profile. Furthermore, metamorphosis offers the advantage of studying a self
contained biological system.<br>
<br>
The tobacco hornworm provides a suitable species to study the effect of an altered
gravitational environment on invertebrate development. This species has been one of the
most thoroughly investigated organisms in a variety of aspects of insect biology. Manduca
sexta pharate adults can provide significant amounts of relevant material, for
investigating metabolic aspects of adult development. During the wandering stage, which
occurs immediately following cessation of larval feeding, the larvae burrow into the soil
to form a pupation chamber. Despite burrowing down 25 to 30 cm, the larvae reorient
themselves to a slightly heads-up vertical position. Since light and temperature are not
factors in this process, they must sense the gravity vector.<br>
<br>
Ground based studies on altered orientation of tobacco hornworm (Manduca sexta) pupae,
relative to gravitational field, showed changes of some amino acids, rate of adult
development, and flight muscles. These parameters are dependent on ecdysone levels, which
are elevated when the insects reorient into a heads-up vertical position. This study was
undertaken to examine the effects of microgravity on tobacco hornworm ecdysone release and
subsequent development. It was hypothesized that the virtual absence of gravity might have
an adverse effect on the "open" circulation system by reducing flow of the
hemolymph through the body, which would also reduce the appearance of ecdysteroid.<p>APPROACH:<p>
Larvae that were one or two days into the wandering stage were shipped to KSC. They were
washed with sterile water upon arrival. Under aseptic conditions, insects were selected
after pupation, as early in development as possible. The pupae were wrapped in tissue and
placed in individual Lexan tubes. The tubes were closed with sterile cotton and attached
to petri dishes with Velcro. <br>
<br>
Fifty-four pupae were loaded into three BRIC-100 canisters ( 18 in each canister) and
placed in a Shuttle Middeck locker. The experiment was launched aboard the STS-77 mission
on May 19, 1996. Ground controls were conducted on a 24-hour delay in the Orbiter
Environmental Simulator (OES) with conditions (temperature, % relative humidity, and
carbon dioxide levels) similar to those experienced by samples during flight.<br>
<br>
The BRIC -07 experiment was totally passive with no crew interaction or inflight
manipulations. After 10 days on orbit, the Shuttle landed at the Kennedy Space Center on
May 29, 1996. The canisters were recovered approximately four hours after landing and
ground controls were "recovered" from the OES 24 hours later.<br>
<br>
Measurements included: 1) development of all insects during flight, 2) analysis of
hemolymph, obtained from half the insects postflight, for ecdysteroid, amino acid, urea,
ammonia and trehalose concentrations, 3) continuing development of the remaining insects
to adult (moths), and 4) analysis of flight muscle protein content of the adults. All
samples were shipped to the University of Arizona the day after landing for further
analysis.<p>RESULTS:<p>
Spaceflight caused development to be slowed by about 15%. Flight pupae lost less weight
than controls, which is consistent with slower development. The flight insects required 6%
longer time to become moths even though only about half of their adult development
occurred during space flight. Postflight, the rate of development was normal. Spaceflight
caused a decrease in total amino acids as a result of a decrease in the concentrations of
isoleucine, urea, cysteine, methionine, aspartic acid, threonine and glutamic
acid+glutamine. Insects subjected to weightlessness and allowed to complete development
postflight showed an 11% lower protein content and a 10% smaller protein concentration of
their flight muscle, indicating that muscle growth was diminished. The concentration of
trehalose in the hemolymph was not affected by space flight. The timing of this flight
study was subsequent to the peak period of ecdysone release. Despite the very low levels
of ecdysone in these insects, a trend towards less ecdysone was detected in flight
insects. The investigators speculate that without gravity, flow of the hemolymph away from
the head region was slower, thereby reducing ecdysone availability and slowing
development.</p>
